# Why save at all?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What is the price of having something now instead of later? How does the interest rate enter the choice?"
next: {"title":"Should you spend a bonus and a raise differently?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/81-should-you-spend-a-bonus-and-a-raise.cast"}

## The price of now
sources:
    - id: thaler1981
      title: Some empirical evidence on dynamic inconsistency
      authors: Richard Thaler
      year: 1981
      finding: 'Median answers: to be as attractive as $15 now, people wanted $20 in one month, $50 in one year, $100 in ten years (as summarised in Frederick, Loewenstein & O''Donoghue 2002, Journal of Economic Literature)'
    - id: yalebond
      title: 'A living artifact from the Dutch Golden Age: Yale''s 367-year-old water bond still pays interest (Yale News, 22 Sept 2015)'
      authors: Yale News
      year: 2015
      finding: A 1648 perpetual bond of the Lekdijk Bovendams water board, held by Yale's Beinecke Library, still pays interest (about EUR 136 collected in 2015)
      url: https://news.yale.edu/2015/09/22/living-artifact-dutch-golden-age-yale-s-367-year-old-water-bond-still-pays-interest

    box now "$15 today" x 220 y 530 width 360 height 150 icon ["dollar banknote","money"] font_size 40 hidden true
    box later "In a year: ?" x 780 y 530 width 360 height 150 icon ["calendar"] font_size 40 hidden true
    arrow swap #8f887c from.anchor right from.ref now to.anchor left to.ref later hidden true
    text swaplabel "price: 1 + r" above swap gap 14 #b5482e font_size 32 hidden true
    scale wait cites ["thaler1981"] x 110 y 330 width 630 label "least you'd take in a year ($)" min 15 max 100 value 50 ticks 5 hidden true
    text bank "savings account: + a few %" #2f6b8f x 500 y 225 font_size 34 hidden true
    portrait thaler x 880 y 330 width 160 of "Richard Thaler" cameo true hidden true
    scratch dollar work [{"tex":"\\$1 \\text{ now} \\rightarrow \\$1.05 \\text{ next year}"},{"tex":"r = 5\\%"}] x 500 y 330 font_size 36 hidden true
    box bond "1648 bond: still paying" cites ["yalebond"] x 500 y 330 width 520 height 130 icon ["scroll"] font_size 36 hidden true
    box market "market: r" #2f6b8f x 270 y 330 width 380 height 140 icon ["bank"] font_size 38 hidden true
    box you "you: impatience" #b5482e x 730 y 330 width 380 height 140 icon ["hourglass"] font_size 38 hidden true
    text rule1 "r > impatience → save" x 500 y 215 font_size 40 hidden true

    card title "Why save at all?"

Why save at all? Why give up something you could enjoy today?
    draw now

B: Let's put a number on it. Fifteen dollars now, or more money a year from now.
    draw swap later

    draw wait

How much more would it take before you'd wait?
    ask "What is the least you'd take in a year instead of $15 today? Click the line." on wait store g default "20"
        right: You said {g} dollars; the middle answer in one survey was {g.true}.
        wrong: You said {g} dollars; the middle answer in one survey was {g.true}.

(@draw thaler@) In 1981 Richard Thaler asked people exactly this. The middle answer was fifty dollars: more than three times as much.

    erase thaler

B: More than three times! A savings account pays a few percent a year.
    draw bank

So by their own answers, hardly anyone should ever save. And yet people save, year after year.
    highlight wait

    erase wait bank

B: Then what are they buying when they save?
    highlight later

Spending later. Saving is a purchase: you pay with dollars now, and get dollars later.
    highlight swap effect underline

B: And the price of that purchase is the interest rate.
    draw swaplabel

B: At r of five percent, a dollar today buys a dollar and five cents next year.
    draw dollar_box dollar_line_1 dollar_line_2

    erase dollar

Markets have set this price for centuries. Yale owns a Dutch water-board bond from 1648 that still pays interest.
    draw bond

    erase bond

    draw market

You carry a second price inside you: how much later you need before you'd give up a little now.
    draw you

Economists call it time preference; plain words say impatience.
    highlight you

Saving is where the two prices meet. When the market pays mor (@draw rule1@)e for waiting than your impatience asks, you save;

when it pays less, you borrow.
    text rule2 "r < impatience → borrow" x 500 y 160 font_size 40

    pause 0.5

## Mia's budget line
domain: {"x":[0,130],"y":[0,125]}
vars:
    r:
      value: 0.1
      min: 0
      max: 0.3
    b:
      value: 1
      fixed: true
    pr:
      expr: 1 + r
    W:
      expr: 50 + 50/(1 + r)
    ys:
      expr: 50 + 50*(1 + r)
    k:
      expr: (b*(1 + r))^2
    c1:
      expr: W/(1 + k/(1 + r))
    c2:
      expr: k*W/(1 + k/(1 + r))
    U:
      expr: sqrt(c1) + b*sqrt(c2)
    sv:
      expr: 50 - c1
sources:
    - id: fisher1930
      title: The Theory of Interest
      authors: Irving Fisher
      year: 1930
      finding: Explains interest by impatience (time preference) and investment opportunity; draws the two-period choice between income now and income later

    axes ax x_label "today ($k)" y_label "next year ($k)" cross corner ticks.x [0,50,100] ticks.y [0,50,100] hidden true
    dot E #3d3833 at.x 50 at.y 50 hidden true
    label l_E "income 50 / 50" right attach_to E font_size 28 hidden true
    dot save_end #2f6b8f at.x 0 at.y 105 bind {"at.y":"ys"} hidden true
    label l_save "save all: {ys:0}" right #2f6b8f attach_to save_end font_size 28 hidden true
    dot borrow_end #b5482e at.x 95.45 at.y 0 bind {"at.x":"W"} hidden true
    label l_borrow "borrow all: {W:0}" above-right #b5482e attach_to borrow_end font_size 28 hidden true
    curve budget thick #3d3833 expr "50 + (1 + r)*(50 - x)" x_from 0 x_to 95.45 bind.x_to W hidden true
    curve budget_old dashed #8f887c expr "50 + 1.1*(50 - x)" x_from 0 x_to 95.45 style.opacity 0.6 style.stroke_width 2 hidden true
    label l_budget "budget line" below-left attach_to budget font_size 28 hidden true
    label l_slope "slope −(1 + r) = −{pr:2}" above-right #b5482e attach_to budget font_size 28 hidden true
    portrait fisher x 860 y 480 width 180 of "Irving Fisher" cameo true hidden true
    curve ic thick #8a5fa8 expr "((U - sqrt(x))/b)^2" x_from 12 x_to 130 hidden true
    label l_ic "equally liked" right #8a5fa8 attach_to ic font_size 28 hidden true
    dot mia #8a5fa8 at.x 45.45 at.y 55 bind {"at.x":"c1","at.y":"c2"} hidden true
    label l_mia "Mia saves {sv:1}k" left #8a5fa8 attach_to mia font_size 28 hidden true
    scratch mathcard top-right work [{"tex":"c_2 = I_2 + (1+r)(I_1 - c_1)"},{"tex":"c_1 + c_2/(1+r) = I_1 + I_2/(1+r)"},{"tex":"\\$1 \\text{ next year} = \\$1/(1+r) \\text{ today}"}] font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "Mia's budget line"

Two prices, then: the market's and your own. One picture holds them both.
    draw ax

Take Mia's bakery in Harbourtown. Say it earns fifty thousand dollars this year and fifty next year.
    draw E l_E

B: Spend each year's money as it comes, and she sits right here.
    highlight E effect circle

Or save all of it. At an illustrative ten percent, fifty thousand grows to fifty-five, so next year she has a hundred and five.
    draw save_end l_save

B: Or borrow against next year. The bank lends her about forty-five thousand now, which grows to fifty by repayment day: ninety-five today, nothing later.
    draw borrow_end l_borrow

Join the points. Irving Fisher drew this in 1930: the intertemporal budget line, every split between today and next year that she can afford.
    draw budget fisher

    erase fisher

    draw l_budget

Its slope is the price of now. Each extra dollar today costs her a dollar ten next year.
    draw l_slope

    erase l_slope

Now her tastes. This curve joins the splits she likes equally well, and its steepness is her impatience.
    draw ic l_ic

B: She lands where the curve just touches the line: there, her impatience equals the interest rate. Mia is patient, so she saves about four and a half thousand.
    draw mia l_mia

    pause 0.5

Now the bank raises its rate.
    highlight budget

    ask "Which point must stay on the new line? Tap it." choose ["E","save_end","borrow_end"] answer E voice b
        right: Her income point: spending each year's money as it comes needs no bank, whatever the rate.
        wrong: The split that needs no bank stays put: here, her income point.

    show budget_old

So the line turns round her income point. At twenty-five percent it is steeper: now is dearer, later cheaper.
    animate r 0.25 duration 3

Your turn: drag the interest rate and watch where Mia lands. She saves more as it rises; but a higher rate also makes a saver richer, and for some savers that pull wins.
    explore params ["r"]

    erase budget_old

    animate r 0.1 duration 1.5

    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"after"}] then after

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq store m default "Skip it" stage own

@math

    erase mathq

Next year she spends her income plus her savings with interest: c two equals I two plus one plus r times what she saved.
    draw mathcard_box mathcard_line_1

Divide by one plus r: everything she spends, valued in today's dollars, equals everything she earns, valued in today's dollars.
    draw mathcard_line_2

A dollar next year is worth one over one plus r today. That is a present value.
    draw mathcard_line_3

    erase mathcard

@after

    erase mathq

So the interest rate sets the slope of the line, impatience shapes the curve, and saving is where they touch.
    highlight budget ic mia

## Is impatience one number?
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: 'Patient and precise: the bank would like a word.'
    good: Most of the price of now, priced right.
    poor: Spend a minute now; it pays later.
    none: Saving the questions for later? Fair enough.
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - 1 month
      - 1 year
      - 10 years
    values:
      - 20
      - 50
      - 100
    value_labels: true
    value_format:
      prefix: $
      decimals: 0
    bar_colors: same
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 120
    y_label: Wanted later instead of $15 now
    box:
      x: 60
      'y': 170
      w: 580
      h: 470
    reference:
      value: 15
      label: ' '
      color: grey
sources:
    - id: thaler1981
      title: 'Some empirical evidence on dynamic inconsistency (Economics Letters 8: 201–207)'
      authors: Richard Thaler
      year: 1981
      finding: 'Median amounts wanted instead of $15 now: $20 in one month, $50 in one year, $100 in ten years — implied yearly discount rates fall with the length of the wait'
    - id: flo2002
      title: 'Time Discounting and Time Preference: A Critical Review (Journal of Economic Literature 40(2))'
      authors: Shane Frederick, George Loewenstein, Ted O'Donoghue
      year: 2002
      finding: 'Summarises Thaler (1981) and many later studies: implied discount rates tend to fall as the delay grows; money-based estimates are confounded by trust, uncertainty and market rates'
    - id: cfpb
      title: What is a payday loan? / proposed rule on payday debt traps
      authors: Consumer Financial Protection Bureau
      year: 2016
      finding: A typical two-week payday loan with a $15 fee per $100 equals an APR of almost 400 percent
    - id: jyske2019
      title: Danish bank is offering 10-year mortgages with negative interest rates (CNBC, 12 Aug 2019)
      authors: CNBC
      year: 2019
      finding: Jyske Bank offered 10-year fixed mortgages at −0.5% before fees
      url: https://www.cnbc.com/2019/08/12/danish-bank-is-offering-10-year-mortgages-with-negative-interest-rates.html

    text rate "10 years: ≈ 20% a year" #2f6b8f x 810 y 560 font_size 32 hidden true
    text steady "one steady rate?" x 810 y 480 font_size 34 hidden true
    mark strike around steady kind strike hidden true
    box trust "trust?" x 810 y 370 width 240 height 110 icon ["handshake"] font_size 32 hidden true
    text s1 "now > later: consensus" #3d3833 x 810 y 380 font_size 32 hidden true
    text s2 "one steady rate: contested" #b5482e x 810 y 320 font_size 32 hidden true
    text ref_t "$15 today" #8f887c x 715 y 250 font_size 26 hidden true

    card title "Is impatience one number?"

B: Patience and price, meeting at one tidy point. But that tidy point treats Mia's impatience as one steady number. Is it?
    draw axes reference_1 ref_t

Thaler asked about other waits too. Instead of fifteen dollars now, people wanted twenty in a month, and fifty in a year.
    draw bar_1 bar_2

What did they want for waiting ten years?
    ask "What did people want for waiting ten years? Drag the bar." on bar_3 store g relative true default "30"
        right: You said {g}; they wanted {g.true}.
        wrong: You said {g}; they wanted {g.true}.

B: A hundred dollars. A month's wait cost a third more; a whole decade, under seven times as much.
    highlight bar_1 bar_3

Per year, that is ferocious impatience over a month and only about twenty percent a year over a decade.
    draw rate

So the nearer the wait, the steeper the impatience.
    draw steady

One steady rate can't describe that.
    draw strike

B: Careful, though. Maybe people just didn't trust a stranger to pay them in ten years.
    draw trust

Fair. Questions about money mix impatience with trust, and with what you could earn or borrow elsewhere. Still, the pattern keeps turning up.
    highlight trust

    erase trust rate

    erase steady strike

How sure are we? That people value now above later: consensus.
    draw s1

That one steady rate describes it: contested.
    draw s2

So why save at all? Because later has a price, the interest rate, and so does your patience. You save when the market pays more for waiting than waiting costs you.
    highlight bar_1 bar_2 bar_3

    quiz "A payday lender charges $15 per $100 for two weeks, almost 400% a year. What does someone who takes the loan reveal?" intro "Three new cases."
        right: Money now is worth a lot to them: borrowing at almost 400% a year only pays if now matters even more.
        wrong: Borrowing at 400% says it: here, money now is worth a lot to them.
        + Money now is worth a lot to them right now
        * Interest rates don't matter to them
        * They prefer later to now

    quiz "In 2019 a Danish bank lent for ten years at −0.5% a year. Ignoring inflation and fees, what did $100 of spending now cost in dollars one year later?" voice b
        right: $99.50: below zero, the price of now drops below one, and the budget line is flatter than one for one.
        wrong: Below zero, the price of now, 1 + r, drops below one: here, $99.50.
        + $99.50
        * $100.50
        * $100

    quiz "Two neighbours with the same incomes face the same bank. Tor saves and Siv borrows. What differs?"
        right: Their time preference: same incomes and same rate give the same line, so only the curves, their impatience, differ.
        wrong: Same incomes and same bank give the same line, so only their tastes are left: here, their time preference.
        + Their time preference
        * Their interest rate
        * Their budget lines

B: But Mia's income was the same both years, and real incomes rarely are. Should a one-off bonus be spent like a raise? That is the next question.
    highlight s1 s2

## Where next
